and CRM are listed on each agent's **Tools** tab. Agent tools and run modes explains how to choose which of those actions wait for your approval."],[343,"The autonomy dropdown under the message box opens on **Ask before acting**, so Olivia aligns on her approach before she takes any action. That setting is one of the points covered in How the Olivia agent compares, below."],[469,"The brief is built to fit on one screen and take about 60 seconds to read, as described under Before a meeting. How this setup lines up against other deal tools is covered in How Oliv AI compares for account executives, below."],[95,"The catalog is the same whichever agent you opened it from. It doesn't reflect the tools that agent already has."],[71,"The common answer is a single switch for the whole agent. Teams often keep it read-only for weeks and paste its suggestions into the CRM by hand. That probably feels safe. It also means the agent saves little time, and the pile of suggestions grows with every rep added."],[151,"The daily rhythm"],[583,"The deal brief is designed to fit one screen and take about 60 seconds to read. It has four parts:"],[412,"The Deal Driver agent docs: the manager's view of the same pipeline."],[520,"The Deal Driver agent: what your manager sees from the same deals."],[230,"The Deal Driver bundle includes meeting-level support for individual reps."],[11,"The Oliv AI approach to agent tools and run modes"],[542,"The Oliv AI approach to meeting preparation"],[430,"The Oliv AI approach to running deals"],[158,"The Oliv AI approach to the Deal Driver agent"],[288,"The Oliv AI approach to the Olivia agent"],[408,"The Oliver agent: the super agent that sets the rules Olivia works within."],[271,"The Olivia agent docs: the front-facing agent reps work with."],[519,"The Olivia agent: the agent you ask for prep, follow-ups and next steps."],[40,"The Overview says Human-Reviewed, but the Tools tab says Auto Run. Which is right?"],[17,"The Overview tab"],[97,"The process steps on the **Instructions** tab name the tools each step uses. Pressing a tool chip there takes you to that tool's card, which is the quickest way to check the mode of a tool a step depends on."],[621,"The qualification status in each brief follows the framework your team runs, whether that's MEDDPICC, BANT, SPICED, MEDPICS or your own. See how Oliv AI scores calls against your sales methodology."],[213,"The raw signals already exist as the team works. Meetings get held, next steps get set, close dates slip and fields stay blank. What's missing is something that reads those signals every day and rolls them up on a schedule."],[617,"The research brief lands 30 minutes before the meeting, and the full detail view stays in the platform for anyone who wants more. How other platforms prepare reps for a first meeting is covered in How meeting preparation compares, below."],[72,"The risk isn't spread evenly across the agent. It sits in the handful of actions that write to shared records or reach customers. Reading a record can't break anything, so it makes sense to hold back only the actions that can."],[51,"The run mode is the second half. It sits on each tool card as a dropdown, and it applies to that one tool on that one agent. Changing it on one tool changes nothing else."],[290,"The suggestion cards"],[79,"The tab has two columns. A sidebar down the left holds **Add Tool** above the list of the agent's tools. The main pane on the right shows the same tools as cards, in the same order. Press a tool's name in the sidebar and its card scrolls into view."],[55,"The tool catalog groups actions by the system they come from. These are the same apps Oliv AI works alongside, from your CRM to your inbox."],[212,"The usual method is a CRM export, a message to each rep asking for an update, and a skim of recent call notes. It can easily take an evening. Even then, the picture is only as current as the last field each rep remembered to fill in."],[614,"The usual prep is a quick tour of LinkedIn, the company's website and a news search, plus any notes in the CRM. Reps who do this well tend to spend 15 to 20 minutes per contact. On a full day that time often isn't there, so the research gets skimmed or skipped."],[466,"The usual routine is to skim old notes in the few minutes between calls. Each follow-up then gets written from memory at the end of the day, and CRM fields wait until Friday. By then some details have probably faded, so the record shows less than the calls did. The next meeting on each deal may start from a thinner picture as a result."],[152,"The weekly rhythm"],[75,"There are three ways in:"],[582,"There's nothing to request. Oliv AI's Meeting Assistant agent sends the brief automatically before every meeting linked to an active opportunity, 30 minutes ahead of the start time. Oliv AI's Olivia agent works through Slack, Teams or the Oliv AI product, so prep shows up where you already work."],[623,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the briefs are used."],[100,"These are typical patterns, shown to illustrate how the settings are used."],[292,"Things you can hand her"],[211,"Think of a regional sales leader with eight account executives and about forty open opportunities. Every Monday she walks the VP of Sales through the region's pipeline. On Friday she starts working out which of those forty deals are really moving."],[645,"Thirty minutes before the meeting. You don't need to request it; it's sent automatically for meetings linked to an active opportunity."],[69,"This matters most to the people who own the systems agents write to: revenue operations teams and CRM admins. It matters at rollout, when a team first lets an agent change records or send mail. It matters again each time an agent gains a new tool, because a tool that stores no mode starts on **Auto Run**."],[612,"This matters to sales leaders whose teams run dozens of calls a week. It matters just as much to SDR and BDR leads whose reps book first meetings back to back. It counts most when calendars fill up, because prep is usually the first thing to go."],[647,"Three bullets on the company and the person, plus an icebreaker. Oliv AI's Olivia agent sends it 30 minutes before the meeting."],[490,"To work a stuck deal, tell Oliv AI's Olivia agent something like \"This deal has been stuck for three weeks.\" She engages Oliv AI's Re-Activator agent and surfaces a re-engagement strategy, as covered in the things you can hand Olivia. For how deals slip in the first place, read why deal views miss half the picture."],[50,"Tools are what turn an agent from something that reads into something that acts. Each tool has a name, an icon and a one-sentence summary of what it does, and the agents described in the product hold between 3 and 8 tools each. The actions cover reading records, creating and updating them, logging calls, meetings and emails, sending and drafting email, managing calendar events and posting to channels."],[63,"Tools decide what an agent may do in your systems, and run modes decide how freely it does it. Together they're where you read and set an agent's permission to act. You make that call one action at a time, not once for the whole agent."],[137,"Treat the **Tools** tab as the control. The **Overview** headings come from a separately stored list and can disagree with the dropdowns."],[595,"Turn each highlighted qualification gap into a question for the call."],[420,"Use cases"],[264,"Visibility tools show you the pipeline. Deal Driver tells you when to look, what to pay attention to and what to do. Many teams run both."],[172,"VPs of Sales and regional heads"],[348,"Watch her work. Her row shows *Thinking...* beside her picture, with a card listing each step as she takes it."],[182,"We already have a pipeline visibility tool. Do we still need Deal Driver?"],[4,"What agent tools and run modes are"],[2,"What an Oliv AI agent's tools are, how to set each tool's run mode to Auto Run, Requires Approval 